    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) are used to treat many hematologic diseases and cancer. However, there is an overall shortage of HSCs available for patient therapy and current standard methods to multiply HSCs in vitro has limited success. Thus, a need to develop methods to maintain HSC stemness is critical. The bone marrow niche naturally maintains and allows self-renewal of HSCs in vivo, and we report the creation of a co-culture system that sufficiently mimics the bone marrow niche condition in vitro to increase preservation of HSC pluripotency ex vivo. First, we determined mesenchymal stem cells (MSCs) maintain the hematopoietic progenitor population with greater efficiency than osteoblasts, and identify MSCs as a candidate cell to test the co-culture efficacy of our culture system. Then, we utilized the novel gelatin-based microribbon material to develop a HSC and MSC co-culture matrix and test the system to establish assays to evaluate co-culture efficacy. Specifically, we use a fibrinogen-based microribbon as it satisfies our co-culture requirements. Overall, we have created a co-culture matrix that both mimics natural bone marrow conditions in 3D structure, cellular components, and growth factors, and can be used to expand HSCs in future research or clinical applications.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
